WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.295
RMF involves many stakeholders.

2
00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:19.017
Effective risk management depends on collaboration between system owners, security professionals, engineers, architects, assessors, privacy personnel, executives, authorizing officials, operations teams, and sometimes mission or business owners.

3
00:00:19.017 --> 00:00:28.147
System owners are typically responsible for understanding the system, its purpose, its boundaries, its users, and its control responsibilities.

4
00:00:28.147 --> 00:00:34.792
They help coordinate the information needed for categorization, control selection, implementation, and monitoring.

5
00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:45.558
Security and privacy professionals help interpret requirements, guide control selection, support assessment preparation, manage findings, and advise on risk.

6
00:00:45.558 --> 00:00:51.924
Engineers and architects turn many of those requirements into working technical and operational controls.

7
00:00:51.924 --> 00:00:55.805
Assessors evaluate control implementation and effectiveness.

8
00:00:55.805 --> 00:01:02.440
They provide findings that help the organization understand strengths, weaknesses, and areas needing remediation.

9
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:08.168
Authorizing officials or accountable leaders make risk decisions based on the available evidence.

10
00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:14.464
Their role is important because risk acceptance is a leadership responsibility, not only a technical one.

11
00:01:14.464 --> 00:01:19.582
Clear responsibilities prevent RMF from becoming a confusing paperwork exercise.

12
00:01:19.582 --> 00:01:27.884
When everyone knows what they own, evidence is easier to collect, controls are easier to maintain, and risk decisions are better informed.

13
00:01:27.884 --> 00:01:32.024
For individual employees, the key takeaway is shared responsibility.

14
00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:41.463
You may not own the entire RMF process, but your work may support controls, evidence, monitoring, remediation, or operational decisions.

15
00:01:41.463 --> 00:01:44.167
Clear role definition also reduces friction.

16
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:52.958
People can respond faster when they know who owns a control, who approves a change, who tracks a finding, and who needs to be informed when risk changes.
